     <h1>Exercise Name: Standing Dumbbell Hammer Curl</h1>


 <b>Exercise Information</b>
            <p><strong>Exercise Name:</strong> Standing Dumbbell Hammer Curl</p>
            <p><strong>Target Muscles:</strong> Brachialis (deep muscle under the bicep) and Brachioradialis (upper forearm)</p>
            <p><strong>Secondary Muscles:</strong> Biceps (Biceps Brachii), Forearm Flexors</p>
        
<b><u><h4>Exercise Overview</h4></u></b>
This 3D anatomical visualization demonstrates Standing Dumbbell Hammer Curls. The video highlights both biceps and forearms (Brachialis and Brachioradialis) in bright glowing colors, illustrating how maintaining a neutral grip (palms facing each other) shifts emphasis to the deeper arm muscles and upper forearms.
      
 Exercise Name: Standing Dumbbell Hammer Curl
 Target Muscles: Brachialis (deep muscle under the bicep) and Brachioradialis (upper forearm)
 Secondary Muscles: Biceps (Biceps Brachii), Forearm Flexors
</div>
    </div>

<pre>
<h1>

How to Perform (Step-by-Step)    </h1>
 <b><u><h4>1.Setup:</h4></u></b> Stand upright with feet shoulder-width apart, holding a dumbbell in each hand by your sides with your palms facing each other (neutral grip).
 <b><u><h4>2.Curling Phase:</h4></u></b> Keep your upper arms stationary and elbows tucked close to your torso. Exhale as you curl both dumbbells upward toward your shoulders while maintaining the neutral grip throughout the movement.
 <b><u><h4>3.Peak Squeeze:</h4></u></b> Pause for a split second at top shoulder height, firmly contracting your brachialis and forearm muscles.
 <b><u><h4>4.Lowering Phase:</h4></u></b> Inhale as you slowly lower the dumbbells back down under control to the starting position.



 <h1>Key Benefits</h1>

 <b>◉Builds Arm Thickness:</b> Directs load onto the Brachialis, pushing the bicep upward to give arms a thicker, fuller look from the front and side.
<b>◉ Forearm & Grip Strength:</b> Targets the Brachioradialis, strengthening the upper forearms and improving overall grip strength.
<b>◉ Elbow Joint Safety:</b> The neutral wrist position places significantly less strain on your wrist and elbow joints compared to traditional underhand curls.



 <h1>Recommended Sets & Reps</h1>

| Goal | Sets | Reps | Rest |
|  |  |  |  |
| Muscle Building (Hypertrophy) | 3 – 4 | 10 – 12 | 60 – 90 seconds |
| Endurance / Tone | 3 – 4 | 12 – 15 | 45 – 60 seconds |
| Strength | 4 – 5 | 6 – 8 | 2 minutes |

Avoid swinging your torso or using momentum to lift the weights—keep your hips still and focus purely on arm contraction.
